# Copy to .env on each machine and choose a strong local password. NEVER commit .env.
#
#   cp ascrybe.env.example .env
#   cp ascrybe.config.example.json ascrybe.config.json
#
# .env holds SECRETS and host-specific ports. ascrybe.config.json holds the runtime
# CONTRACT (model roles, bounds, dashboard address) and only NAMES these variables rather
# than containing their values, so the config is safe to read and share while credentials
# stay here.
#
# Model provider authentication is NOT here: Pi owns it. Run `pi --list-models` and confirm
# every model identifier in ascrybe.config.json appears in that output before any paid
# run; the tools hard-abort when a configured role is unavailable.
#
# --- Tuning the paid read ---
#
# These values live in ascrybe.config.json, not here: JSON cannot carry comments, so the
# reasoning for them is kept in this file. Every model role takes the same five bounds.
#
# window_bytes -- the size of ONE paid unit of document text.
#
#     windows        ~= corpus_bytes / window_bytes
#     claims/window  ~= window_bytes x the corpus's claim density
#     cost           ~= windows x (fixed prompt overhead + the window itself)
#
#   Total bytes read barely moves with window size. The FIXED per-window overhead does, so
#   larger windows spend less on overhead -- until they hit the claim cap.
#
#   MAX_CLAIMS_PER_WINDOW is 30, hardcoded in tools/semantic-claim-extractor.mjs. A window
#   proposing more is truncated, and its denominator is capped rather than closed. Measured at
#   window_bytes=4000 across 6,815 windows: 122,587 proposals, 18.0 per window, and 207 windows
#   (3.0%) already at the cap. Doubling the window drives the mean toward 30, at which point the
#   cap rather than the document decides what the graph contains.
#
#   So the two move together. Raising window_bytes alone buys overhead savings with coverage,
#   and the only trace is windows_at_claim_cap in the run receipt. Read it after any change; it
#   should stay near zero.
#
# concurrency -- windows in flight at once (1..256).
#
#   Retries are deliberately suppressed as unsafe, so a rate-limited window is LOST, not retried.
#   Throughput and coverage trade directly against each other here. Raising this is only free
#   while the provider keeps up; past that it silently buys speed with missing claims.
#
# max_event_bytes / max_answer_bytes -- hard bounds on one model response.
#
#   max_answer_bytes <= max_event_bytes is validated. The ceiling above both is V8's, not ours:
#
#     buffer.constants.MAX_STRING_LENGTH = 536,870,888 bytes   (64-bit Node)
#
#   Note that 512 MiB is 536,870,912 -- twenty-four bytes ABOVE that ceiling. Configuring the
#   round number killed a corpus run at window 27 with `RangeError: Invalid string length`,
#   because the accumulator raced the two limits and a chunk boundary decided whether the stream
#   was recorded as over-budget or threw. A bound above the capacity of the type holding it is
#   not a bound.
#
#   The example config still ships 536,870,912 ON PURPOSE, and a test asserts that it does. The
#   runner clamps retention to MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1024, and that clamp keeps a live witness only
#   while some shipped value exceeds the ceiling. Lowering the example would delete the evidence
#   that the clamp is load-bearing. Byte accounting still uses the configured cap, so what the
#   receipt reports is unchanged; only what is retained in memory is clamped.
#
# timeout_ms -- per-window wall clock. A window that times out is lost on the same terms as a
#   rate-limited one, so this bounds cost only by bounding coverage.
#
# thinking -- pinned per role, never null. `null` does not mean "off"; it means the flag is never
#   sent and the provider's default decides, which moved cost 5x between two weeks. For
#   schema-constrained extraction, `off` measured 82% cheaper with substantively the same claims.

# --- External generated-state custody ---
# Optional. Without this, state:init uses XDG_STATE_HOME/estate-map-runner or
# ~/.local/state/estate-map-runner. This path must be outside the checkout and Git common dir.
# ASCRYBE_ARTIFACT_ROOT=/absolute/path/to/estate-map-runner-state

# --- Neo4j credentials (consumed via the *_env names in ascrybe.config.json) ---
ASCRYBE_NEO4J_USERNAME=neo4j
ASCRYBE_NEO4J_PASSWORD=replace-with-a-long-local-password
ASCRYBE_NEO4J_URI=http://127.0.0.1:7475

# --- Host port mapping for the packaged Neo4j service (compose.yaml) ---
# Defaults avoid 7474/7687 so this never collides with another Neo4j on the machine.
ASCRYBE_NEO4J_HTTP_PORT=7475
ASCRYBE_NEO4J_BOLT_PORT=7688

# --- Optional Neo4j memory sizing; raise for corpus-scale projections ---
#
# Page cache wants to exceed the store on disk if the whole graph is to stay resident; heap
# carries the transaction, and staging writes a generation in one. Both are the SERVER's memory.
# The client needs its own: a generation is built whole in Node before any of it is written, so a
# corpus-scale projection is bounded by node --max-old-space-size as much as by anything here.
# ASCRYBE_NEO4J_HEAP_INITIAL=1G
# ASCRYBE_NEO4J_HEAP_MAX=4G
# ASCRYBE_NEO4J_PAGECACHE=2G
